description:
Implement mike_trade/execution/fill_processor.py, a FillProcessor that ingests normalized broker trade updates and activities (from the e-phase normalization layer, or simulated SHADOW fills) and applies them to the order FSM and the account ledger exactly once at the application level.

(1) Deduplication: every event carries a provider event/activity id, or a deterministic composite key when the provider has none. Persist the dedupe key in the same transaction as the ledger effect. A duplicate key is a no-op returning DUPLICATE. Fill processing must survive restart: re-ingesting the same activity after a restart changes nothing.
(2) Fill application: for each new fill, compute the incremental filled quantity and price in Decimal, using the cumulative filled_qty when available so that missed or out-of-order partial updates are reconciled without double counting. Update the order's filled_qty and avg fill price, transition through PARTIALLY_FILLED/FILLED via the f.1 FSM, and update the position cycle's actual held quantity and cost basis. For entries, consume the corresponding f.2 cash reservation proportionally. On a terminal zero-fill or partial-then-cancel/expire, release the unused remainder.
(3) Status-only events: ACCEPTED/PENDING/NEW never change quantity. A cancel or expire event moves CANCEL_PENDING (or a working state) to CANCELED/EXPIRED and releases reservations for any unfilled quantity. A rejected event releases the full reservation. A fill arriving after a cancel request but before cancel confirmation is applied normally.
(4) Out-of-order events: an older status arriving after a newer one is recorded but not applied, per the f.1 FSM. Fills are always applied by cumulative quantity, never by event order. Fills for an unknown client_order_id are recorded as an UNMANAGED/reconciliation-drift event that blocks new entries, and are never silently dropped.
(5) Oversell safety: if a sell fill (for example a broker protective stop racing an application EXIT_LONG) would reduce held quantity below zero, clamp nothing. Record a critical reconciliation-drift event, mark the position for reconciliation, and block new entries. Held long quantity is the confirmed sum of buys minus sells, and a position cycle closes only when the remaining quantity reaches exactly zero.
(6) Emit protection-relevant notifications (for example positive-fill-confirmed with the current remaining qty) through an injected callback, for later protective-stop management. Do not implement stop placement here.

Create tests/unit/test_fill_processor.py covering:
- single full fill;
- multiple partial fills;
- partial fill then cancel with reservation release;
- rejected and canceled zero-fill orders;
- a duplicate event id producing no double count;
- re-ingestion after a simulated restart;
- out-of-order cumulative fills;
- an ACCEPTED event not changing quantity;
- a fill after a cancel request;
- a protective stop fill plus an exit fill exceeding held qty, producing drift and an entry block with no negative position silently created;
- a fill for an unknown client_order_id producing drift;
- Decimal-only cost basis.

Before editing, inspect: mike_trade/execution/order_fsm.py, mike_trade/execution/intents.py, mike_trade/execution/coordinator.py, mike_trade/brokers/ports.py, mike_trade/brokers/alpaca/updates.py, mike_trade/brokers/alpaca/activities.py, mike_trade/domain/trading.py, mike_trade/domain/protection.py, mike_trade/persistence/journal.py, mike_trade/persistence/repositories.py, mike_trade/persistence/transaction.py, and mike_trade/persistence/projections.py. Bounded decision budget: use existing persistence transaction/journal APIs, and do not alter schema or other modules.
